{"v":1,"agent":"Codex","cli_version":"dev","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-04-21T23:21:27.179Z","content":[{"text":"**Strengths**\n- The implementation stays narrow and cohesive: enrichment logic is isolated in `why.go`, and the summary fallback behavior is covered with a focused table-driven test.\n- `enrichWhyBlocks` deduplicates commit hashes before resolving them, which avoids repeated lookup work for duplicate blame entries.\n\n**Issues**\n\n**Important**\n- `cmd/entire/cli/why.go:101` and `cmd/entire/cli/why.go:129`: `ResolveCommittedReaderForCheckpoint` already returns the committed checkpoint summary, but `loadWhyEntireInfoImpl` ignores it and calls `reader.ReadCommitted` again. Why: this doubles checkpoint I/O on every enrichment and makes the code more fragile if the metadata branch changes between the two reads. Suggested fix: thread the returned `CheckpointSummary` through and use it directly instead of rereading the same checkpoint.\n- `cmd/entire/cli/why_test.go:14`: `TestWhyBlock_PreservesEntireMetadata` is mostly a fixture self-check, not a behavioral test. Why: it can still pass if the production code that produces `whyBlock` regresses, so it adds false confidence. Suggested fix: replace it with a test that exercises `parseWhyBlamePorcelain` or `enrichWhyBlocks`, or remove the literal field reassertions that don’t validate behavior.\n\n**Assessment**\nNeeds changes.","type":"text"}]}
